Bug 1917314 - Don't drop the last occurrence of an all-day repeating event. r=tobyp

Making an event all-day moves DTSTART from the old time of day to
midnight, and CalRecurrenceInfo.onStartDateChange shifts the rule's UNTIL
by that same amount. Adding a sub-day duration to a DATE should be a
no-op, and it is - until the value gets cloned: ICAL.Time normalizes
lazily, and Time#clone() ignores the pending normalization, carrying the
leftover hours into the day rather than dropping them. UNTIL moved back a
day, so the occurrence falling on it was lost.

Fixing this upstream is https://github.com/kewisch/ical.js/issues/1024

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D323618


diff --git a/calendar/base/modules/Ical.sys.mjs b/calendar/base/modules/Ical.sys.mjs
--- a/calendar/base/modules/Ical.sys.mjs
+++ b/calendar/base/modules/Ical.sys.mjs
@@ -1348,16 +1348,23 @@ class Time {
   }
 
   /**
    * Returns a clone of the time object.
    *
    * @return {Time}              The cloned object
    */
   clone() {
+    // Local patch for https://github.com/kewisch/ical.js/issues/1024: flush a
+    // pending normalization, so that cloning a date holding leftover time
+    // components does not carry those over into the day (bug 1917314).
+    if (this._pendingNormalization) {
+      this._normalize();
+      this._pendingNormalization = false;
+    }
     return new Time(this._time, this.zone);
   }
 
   /**
    * Reset the time instance to epoch time
    */
   reset() {
     this.fromData(Time.epochTime);
